        "title": "Round 2 - The Copy in the Tray",
        "narration": "Iris produces the duplicate will from her correspondence tray. The family still inherits. Pell is removed as trustee and must account for unexplained withdrawals. A rumor that Cecily cut off her heirs is now testable against an actual document.\n\nBeatrice brings out the letter she concealed while deciding whether to confront the adviser publicly: \"Pell must return what he has taken, or tomorrow I go to the bank.\" Her argument with Cecily concerned control of her inheritance. Ada admits taking a small household sum to help her brother; it is a different account from the trust.\n\nRead the clues. What could somebody lose through the announcement? A duplicate survives, so stealing the original will cannot change its terms. The scale and recipient of the missing money still need checking.\n\nThe host reads the original neighbors' recorded responses:\nEdmund Pell's recorded response: \"I learned Felix Harcourt borrowed under a false promise without reading the will. I don't trust his disinheritance guess dressed as fact.\"\nBeatrice Harcourt's recorded response: \"I learned Iris Shaw planned to leave without telling us. I found her duplicate will in the correspondence tray. Why keep important papers?\"\nAda March's recorded response: \"I learned Beatrice Harcourt hid Cecily's account letter. After their argument, I wonder whether hiding that warning means money mattered more than reconciliation.\"\nIris Shaw's recorded response: \"I read the duplicate removing Edmund Pell as trustee; Cecily demands repayment before visiting the bank. I see him losing most, not the heirs.\"\nFelix Harcourt's recorded response: \"I learned Ada March delayed her corridor account, fearing investigation. Missing household money makes me suspicious of that delay.\"\n\nSource-grounded supporting comparisons:\nEdmund Pell was removed as trustee by the duplicate will.\nAda March admitted a household shortfall separate from the trust.\nIris Shaw's tray held the duplicate preserving the heirs.\nBeatrice Harcourt produced Cecily’s repayment letter after concealing it.\nFelix Harcourt still inherited enough to address his debts.\nSylvia Grey reported Cecily called the accounts someone else’s purse.\nDr. Rowan Hale faced a complaint among several accountability letters.\nNora Bellamy distinguished the larger withdrawals from her repair allowance.\nJulian Finch reported a bank source’s account of a requested trustee review.\nMarian Vale reported the family could manage its inheritance without Pell.\nJulian’s supplied account names Julian's Bank Contact as his unnamed review source.\nAda’s supplied account mentions household money taken to help Ada's Brother.",
