        "title": "Round 4 - A Sound Is Not a Clock",
        "narration": "Ada demonstrates how the bell mechanism continues sounding after the cord is pulled. Someone already leaving the study can hear it from the corridor. Pell's claim to have heard it elsewhere does not establish where he was when it started.\n\nIris explains that Cecily ordered her to keep copies, and that she planned to leave her job with a reference. Her retained papers are not evidence that she stole the will. The nephew had never read the document he complained about. Cecily also wrote professional complaints, but those letters remained on the desk while the will and financial page disappeared.\n\nHear every clue. Return to the receipt, the folded document and the exit before the bell stopped. Each was introduced before this explanation of the mechanism; no new attacker is being squeezed into the timeline.\n\nThe host reads the original neighbors' recorded responses:\nEdmund Pell's recorded response: \"I heard Ada March say the bell sounds after the pull. Her corridor sighting could challenge an alibi, not time a later attack. I worry her account-investigation fears delayed it.\"\nBeatrice Harcourt's recorded response: \"I read the appointment naming Edmund Pell and the secretary's receipt for his will collection. He denies the meeting. I can't accept hearing a continuing bell elsewhere as an answer.\"\nAda March's recorded response: \"I see Iris Shaw's duplicate will contradicting the inheritance rumor. Keeping papers while preparing to leave looks like theft to me, until I hear Cecily's instructions about copies.\"\nIris Shaw's recorded response: \"I read that Felix Harcourt still gets enough for his debts. The adviser encouraged his complaints before supper. I wonder whether that false rumor needed a public voice.\"\nFelix Harcourt's recorded response: \"I read the duplicate: Beatrice Harcourt still inherits; her hidden letter demands Pell repay money. I see an argument over control, not the adviser's fear of repayment.\"\n\nSource-grounded supporting comparisons:\nEdmund Pell denied the study entry despite the receipt and exit account.\nAda March demonstrated ringing continuing after the bell cord was pulled.\nIris Shaw explained Cecily’s instructions to keep copies.\nBeatrice Harcourt still inherited under the duplicate will.\nFelix Harcourt had complained without reading the will.\nSylvia Grey reported Cecily’s concern about the accounts.\nDr. Rowan Hale's complaint remained while the financial papers disappeared.\nNora Bellamy distinguished the repair allowance from the larger withdrawals.\nJulian Finch had reported the requested trustee review, not a study sighting.\nMarian Vale had reported the planned private meeting.\nThe account attributed to Julian's Bank Contact concerns a review, not a study sighting.\nThe account mentioning Ada's Brother concerns household money, not a study sighting.",
