        "title": "Round 1 - The Bell After the Footsteps",
        "narration": "Cecily goes into her study before the family announcement. The bell sounds. Ada sees Edmund Pell leave the study before its ringing finishes, carrying a folded legal document. Iris enters afterward and finds Cecily dead. The new will is missing; an account page has been torn out.\n\nEarlier Pell signed Iris's receipt book when collecting the will for the meeting. Beatrice knew her mother had a private appointment before the gathering. Pell says he never entered the study and heard the bell from the drawing room. Set his statement beside the receipt and the corridor sighting; do not settle the timing yet.\n\nRead the first clues. Arguments, threatened jobs and debts make the household uneasy. The missing papers and sequence around the body are more specific than a general family quarrel.\n\nThe host reads the original neighbors' recorded responses:\nEdmund Pell's recorded response: \"I learned Iris Shaw found Cecily after the bell; the receipt records an earlier handover of the missing will. I question her document access, but that signature points to another holder.\"\nBeatrice Harcourt's recorded response: \"I heard Ada March was near the study corridor before the alarm, then saw someone leave during ringing. Cecily's account check threatened her job. I wonder why she delayed her account.\"\nAda March's recorded response: \"I heard Felix Harcourt shout before supper that the new will would ruin him; debt notices explain his fear. I want the surviving document, not a shouted rumor.\"\nIris Shaw's recorded response: \"I heard Beatrice Harcourt argue with Cecily about inheritance control this afternoon; a private appointment followed before the alarm. I'm troubled, but when did that meeting happen and those papers disappear?\"\nFelix Harcourt's recorded response: \"I read Edmund Pell's signed will receipt before Cecily's meeting; Ada saw him leave the study with a folded document before the bell stopped. I can't reconcile his denial of entry.\"\n\nSource-grounded supporting comparisons:\nEdmund Pell signed the will receipt and denied entering the study.\nAda March saw Pell leave before the bell stopped.\nIris Shaw found Cecily and recorded the earlier will handover.\nBeatrice Harcourt argued about inheritance control before the private appointment.\nFelix Harcourt complained about a will he had not read.\nSylvia Grey reported Cecily wanted to repay loyalty, not punish her family.\nDr. Rowan Hale reported Cecily was alert before entering the study.\nNora Bellamy described the proposed trust as support for injured workers.\nJulian Finch held an invitation announcing a charitable trust.\nMarian Vale reported Cecily planned a private meeting with Pell.\nNo eyewitness statement by Julian's Bank Contact is supplied for the study sequence.\nNo eyewitness statement by Ada's Brother is supplied for the study sequence.",
