    "title": "The Last Will at Briar House",
    "setting": "Briar House, a fictional New England manufacturing town, 1893. A respectable household hides fierce arguments over money. Inspired loosely by the atmosphere surrounding the Borden case, with invented characters, crime and solution.",
    "intro": "Cecily Harcourt has gathered family, staff and advisers at Briar House for an announcement. She is changing her will and dismissing the person who has been managing her accounts.\n\nA charitable trust will receive much of her fortune. Nobody knows how much remains for the family. The new document waits in her study; the old one is displayed in the drawing room.\n\nAt eight, Cecily says she will fetch the final account book. \"Respectability,\" she remarks, \"is an expensive disguise.\" The front gate is locked for the evening. Everyone has a reason to worry.\n\nThree readers carry the investigation: Edmund Pell, Ada March and Iris Shaw. The host reads every supplied account, including those from absent neighbors. Discuss only released evidence; nobody invents a private history or a new discovery.",
